  "footnote": "Source: calculated from Regulations (EU) 2025/2621 and 2025/2620 and Directive 2023/959. From 2027 the amounts hold the price at €75.36 as a demonstration",
  "notes": "This is what we pay per tonne. All four lines follow the default path, where we do nothing and the declarant files the EU's default values for China. Hot-rolled coil costs about €163.5 per tonne in 2026, or about €1.64 million per 10,000 tonnes exported. It rises every year for two reasons: free allocation falls by law from 97.5% to zero in 2034, and the default-value markup climbs from 10% to 30% from 2028. By 2034 coil costs about €312 per tonne, unwrought aluminium about €294, aluminium plate about €404 and carbon steel bolts about €625. To be clear, the certificate price is held at the first-quarter 2026 level of €75.36 throughout, so the chart shows what the rules alone do. It is not a carbon price forecast. On sensitivity, every €10 on the certificate price moves the 2026 coil cost by about €21.7 per tonne. Our recalculated third-quarter price is €82.32, which the Commission has not yet published."
